  "articlePanels": [
    {"kind":"lede","html":"<p>This is a free <strong>Care Inspectorate self-evaluation template</strong> for a Scottish care service. It is structured around the adult-care key questions the Care Inspectorate uses, so the judgements you make here map to what an inspector assesses. Copy it into your own document, print it, or run it as a live workflow. The template is deliberately simple: for each key question you record the evidence you can see, grade yourself on the six-point scale, and write the action that would move the grade.</p><p>The exact quality indicator wording changes from time to time, so always work from the Care Inspectorate's current published quality framework and the Health and Social Care Standards. Treat this as general information, not regulatory advice.</p>"},
    {"kind":"takeaways","heading":"The short version","html":"<ul><li>This template covers the four adult-care key questions: care and support, setting, staff team, and leadership.</li><li>For each, it prompts the quality indicator area, the evidence to gather, a self-grade on the six-point scale, and an improvement action.</li><li>Grade yourself honestly. A candid weak grade with a clear action reads better than a generous grade you cannot evidence.</li><li>Work from the Care Inspectorate's current published framework and standards, as the exact indicator strings are updated over time.</li><li>Quickler can run this same template as a WhatsApp workflow, capturing evidence at the point you observe it.</li><li>Quickler is not a care record, an eMAR or a care management system. It is the evaluation and evidence layer around them.</li></ul>"},
    {"kind":"default","eyebrow":"Key question 1","heading":"How good is our care and support?","html":"<p>The key question most weighted at inspection. Work from the current published quality indicators for this key question and record honest evidence against each row.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Quality indicator area</th><th>Evidence to gather</th><th>Self-grade (1 to 6)</th><th>Improvement action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>People experience compassion, dignity and respect</td><td>Observed interactions; residents' and families' own words; complaints and how they were resolved</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td>Care and support is right for each person</td><td>Personal plans reviewed and current; outcomes tracked; choices recorded and honoured</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td>Health and wellbeing benefit from care and support</td><td>Nutrition, activity, mental wellbeing evidence; input from health professionals; how deterioration is spotted and acted on</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td>People are safe and protected from harm</td><td>Safeguarding records; risk assessments current; medication handling; accident and incident trends</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr></tbody></table>"},
    {"kind":"default","eyebrow":"Key question 2","heading":"How good is our setting?","html":"<p>Whether the environment supports people's wellbeing, safety and independence. Photograph what you can and note what a visitor would notice.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Quality indicator area</th><th>Evidence to gather</th><th>Self-grade (1 to 6)</th><th>Improvement action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The setting is safe, clean and well maintained</td><td>Cleanliness walkround; maintenance and repair log; infection prevention measures in place</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td>The setting supports wellbeing and independence</td><td>Signage, lighting, quiet and social spaces; access to outdoors; personalisation of rooms</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td>Equipment and premises are suitable</td><td>Equipment servicing records; adaptations meet needs; environment risk assessments current</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr></tbody></table>"},
    {"kind":"default","eyebrow":"Key question 3","heading":"How good is our staff team?","html":"<p>Whether the team is recruited safely, skilled, supported and deployed in the numbers people need.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Quality indicator area</th><th>Evidence to gather</th><th>Self-grade (1 to 6)</th><th>Improvement action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Staff are recruited and inducted safely</td><td>Safer recruitment checks; PVG scheme records; induction completed and evidenced</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td>Staff have the right skills and are supported</td><td>Training matrix current; supervision and appraisal records; registration with the SSSC where required</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td>Staffing levels meet people's needs</td><td>Dependency assessments; rotas against need; how gaps and absence are covered</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr></tbody></table>"},
    {"kind":"default","eyebrow":"Key question 4","heading":"How good is our leadership?","html":"<p>Often where grades turn. Whether leadership is visionary, accountable, and drives its own improvement.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Quality indicator area</th><th>Evidence to gather</th><th>Self-grade (1 to 6)</th><th>Improvement action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Vision, values and culture are strong</td><td>How values are lived day to day; staff and resident views; how concerns are raised and heard</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td>Quality assurance and improvement work</td><td>This self-evaluation itself; audit programme; improvement plan with owners and dates; action taken on findings</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td>Leadership is accountable and developing</td><td>Governance and oversight; how the manager develops the team; response to previous inspection findings</td><td>&nbsp;</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr></tbody></table>"},
    {"kind":"default","eyebrow":"After you fill it in","heading":"Turn the grades into an improvement plan","html":"<p>A completed self-evaluation is only worth the action behind it. Once every row has an honest grade, take every row you graded three or below and turn its improvement action into a task with an owner and a date. That is the improvement plan an inspector wants to see, and it is what raises a grade over time. Filing the completed template and doing nothing changes nothing.</p><p>If you run this template as a Quickler WhatsApp workflow, each low self-grade becomes a flagged action automatically, so the walk turns straight into the plan. The self-evaluation stays the manager's honest judgement; the tool just removes the clipboard and the retyping.</p>"},
    {"kind":"faq","heading":"Questions, answered","html":"<h3>Is there an official Care Inspectorate self-evaluation template?</h3><p>The Care Inspectorate publishes its quality framework and supporting self-evaluation materials, and these are updated from time to time. This page gives a free, general template structured around the adult-care key questions so you can start today, but you should always work from the Care Inspectorate's current published framework and standards for the exact indicator wording. Treat this template as general information, not regulatory advice.</p><h3>How do the self-grades work?</h3><p>Scottish care services are graded on a six-point scale, from one for unsatisfactory up to six for excellent. Grade each row honestly against the current published quality indicators. A candid low grade with a clear improvement action is worth more than a generous grade you cannot evidence.</p><h3>Can I print this template or copy it?</h3><p>Yes. It is free to copy into your own document or print and take round the service. There is no sign-up needed to use it. If you would rather run it without a clipboard, Quickler can deliver the same template as a WhatsApp workflow.</p><h3>How much does Quickler cost if I want to run this as a workflow?</h3><p>Quickler is £20 per active user per month, with a £20 minimum. An active user is someone who completed at least two workflows that month, so dormant users are free and you only pay for who actually works. There is a free tier of 20 reports per month with up to 10 photos per report, so you can run this self-evaluation without paying anything. Quickler is not a care record or care management system; it is the evaluation and evidence layer around them.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/care-inspectorate-self-evaluation-scotland.html\">Care Inspectorate self-evaluation guide</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/care-inspectorate-grades-explained-scotland.html\">Care Inspectorate grades explained</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/care-inspectorate-annual-return-scotland.html\">Care Inspectorate annual return</a></li></ul>"}
